{"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"],"fields":{"title":{"boost":1000.0},"text":{"boost":1.0},"tags":{"boost":1000000.0}}},"docs":[{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/index.html","title":"Overview and preface","text":"Tales and FolkloreThe PanchatantraPañcatantra, in Pūrṇabhadra’s recension of 1199Translated by Arthur W. Ryder <p>Tales and Folklore</p> The Panchatantra <p>Pañcatantra, in Pūrṇabhadra’s recension of 1199</p> <p>Translated by Arthur W. Ryder</p> <p>The five books of animal fables for the education of princes, with their frame stories and verses, in Arthur W. Ryder’s lively translation (1925).</p> Start reading <p>88 chapters</p>"},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/index.html#contents","title":"Contents","text":"<p>Front matter</p> <ul> <li>Translator’s Introduction</li> </ul> <p>Chapters</p> <ul> <li>Introduction</li> </ul> <p>Book I: The Loss of Friends</p> <ul> <li>The Loss of Friends</li> <li>The Wedge-Pulling Monkey</li> <li>The Jackal and the War-Drum</li> <li>Merchant Strong-Tooth</li> <li>Godly and June</li> <li>The Jackal at the Ram-Fight</li> <li>The Weaver’s Wife</li> <li>How the Crow-Hen Killed the Black Snake</li> <li>The Heron That Liked Crab-Meat</li> <li>Numskull and the Rabbit</li> <li>The Weaver Who Loved a Princess</li> <li>The Ungrateful Man</li> <li>Leap and Creep</li> <li>The Blue Jackal</li> <li>Passion and the Owl</li> <li>Ugly’s Trust Abused</li> <li>The Lion and the Carpenter</li> <li>The Plover Who Fought the Ocean</li> <li>Shell-Neck, Slim, and Grim</li> <li>Forethought, Readywit, and Fatalist</li> <li>The Duel between Elephant and Sparrow</li> <li>The Shrewd Old Gander</li> <li>The Lion and the Ram</li> <li>Smart, the Jackal</li> <li>The Monk Who Left His Body Behind</li> <li>The Girl Who Married a Snake</li> <li>Poor Blossom</li> <li>The Unteachable Monkey</li> <li>Right-Mind and Wrong-Mind</li> <li>A Remedy Worse than the Disease</li> <li>The Mice That Ate Iron</li> <li>The Results of Education</li> <li>The Sensible Enemy</li> <li>The Foolish Friend</li> </ul> <p>Book II: The Winning of Friends</p> <ul> <li>The Winning of Friends</li> <li>The Bharunda Birds</li> <li>Gold’s Gloom</li> <li>Mother Shandilee’s Bargain</li> <li>Self-defeating Forethought</li> <li>Mister Duly</li> <li>Soft, the Weaver</li> <li>Hang-Ball and Greedy</li> <li>The Mice That Set Elephants Free</li> <li>Spot’s Captivity</li> </ul> <p>Book III: Crows and Owls</p> <ul> <li>Crows and Owls</li> <li>How the Birds Picked a King</li> <li>How the Rabbit Fooled the Elephant</li> <li>The Cat’s Judgment</li> <li>The Brahman’s Goat</li> <li>The Snake and the Ants</li> <li>The Snake Who Paid Cash</li> <li>The Unsocial Swans</li> <li>The Self-sacrificing Dove</li> <li>The Old Man with the Young Wife</li> <li>The Brahman, the Thief, and the Ghost</li> <li>The Snake in the Prince’s Belly</li> <li>The Gullible Carpenter</li> <li>Mouse-Maid Made Mouse</li> <li>The Bird with Golden Dung</li> <li>The Cave That Talked</li> <li>The Frogs That Rode Snakeback</li> <li>The Butter-blinded Brahman</li> </ul> <p>Book IV: Loss of Gains</p> <ul> <li>Loss of Gains</li> <li>The Monkey and the Crocodile</li> <li>Handsome and Theodore</li> <li>Flop-Ear and Dusty</li> <li>The Potter Militant</li> <li>The Jackal Who Killed No Elephants</li> <li>The Ungrateful Wife</li> <li>King Joy and Secretary Splendor</li> <li>The Ass in the Tiger-Skin</li> <li>The Farmer’s Wife</li> <li>The Pert Hen-Sparrow</li> <li>How Supersmart Ate the Elephant</li> <li>The Dog Who Went Abroad</li> </ul> <p>Book V: Ill-Considered Action</p> <ul> <li>Ill-considered Action</li> <li>The Loyal Mungoose</li> <li>The Four Treasure-Seekers</li> <li>The Lion-Makers</li> <li>Hundred-Wit, Thousand-Wit, and Single-Wit</li> <li>The Musical Donkey</li> <li>Slow, the Weaver</li> <li>The Brahman’s Dream</li> <li>The Unforgiving Monkey</li> <li>The Credulous Fiend</li> <li>The Three-breasted Princess</li> <li>The Fiend Who Washed His Feet</li> </ul>"},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/index.html#about-this-book","title":"About this book","text":"<p>The Pañcatantra, “the five treatises”, a Sanskrit book of statecraft and worldly wisdom told as fables of jackals, lions, crows, mice, monkeys and men, nested story within story, with verses of advice: the Loss of Friends, the Winning of Friends, Crows and Owls, Loss of Gains and Ill-considered Action. The tradition says the Brahman Viṣṇuśarman taught it to three dull princes in six months. Arthur W. Ryder (1877–1938) translated the Jain recension made by Pūrṇabhadra in 1199 (University of Chicago Press, 1925), keeping the verses in verse.</p> <p>From Wikisource · Public domain</p><p>The Panchatantra, translated by Arthur W. Ryder (1877–1938), University of Chicago Press, 1925, as validated on English Wikisource by its contributors. This book is not stored in this library: each chapter is read from Wikisource when it is opened, shown in this library's format, and ends with its own attribution. Licence: Public domain. Modified: converted to this format.</p>"},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/a-remedy-worse-than-the-disease.html","title":"A Remedy Worse than the Disease","text":""},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/crows-and-owls.html","title":"Crows and Owls","text":""},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/flop-ear-and-dusty.html","title":"Flop-Ear and Dusty","text":""},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/forethought-readywit-and-fatalist.html","title":"Forethought, Readywit, and Fatalist","text":""},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/godly-and-june.html","title":"Godly and June","text":""},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/gold-s-gloom.html","title":"Gold’s Gloom","text":""},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/handsome-and-theodore.html","title":"Handsome and Theodore","text":""},{"location":"tales-folklore/panchatantra-ryder/hang-ball-and-greedy.